    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Student-Cultural-Wellbeing---Placement-Support-Officer_JR0000017357": {
      "title": "Student Cultural Wellbeing & Placement Support Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 15 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Continuing | Full-time Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $ 94,456 - $ 100,710 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role As the Student Cultural Wellbeing & Placement Support Officer, you will play an instrumental role in fostering a culturally safe, inclusive and supportive learning environment for students across the College of Medicine and Public Health. Working closely with academic, professional, and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff, you will provide proactive wellbeing support to help students navigate challenging curriculum content and placement experiences, while promoting cultural safety, resilience, engagement and student success. This is an exciting time to join the College of Medicine and Public Health as we continue to invest in student and cultural wellbeing as a key priority area. Key responsibilities of this position include: Providing culturally safe, student-centred wellbeing support, including debriefing, reflective conversations and wellbeing check-ins for students across a range of learning environments. Supporting students before, during and after clinical, rural, remote, community and Aboriginal health placements to enhance preparedness, cultural understanding and engagement. Assisting students experiencing distress, culture shock, racism or culturally unsafe experiences, and facilitating referrals to appropriate University, College and external support services. Collaborating with academic, professional and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander colleagues to strengthen student support pathways, wellbeing initiatives and culturally safe educational practices. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035, through directly contributing to the strategic driver of being Student Centric, ensuring students are supported to engage, thrive and succeed in culturally safe learning and placement environments. About You You are a compassionate and culturally aware professional with experience supporting the wellbeing of diverse individuals and communities. You bring a strong understanding of cultural safety, equity and inclusion, along with the ability to build trusted relationships, respond appropriately to students experiencing distress, and work collaboratively with a broad range of stakeholders to promote positive student outcomes. To be successful in this position, you will have: Experience providing wellbeing, social or emotional support to individuals and groups, with the ability to respond sensitively to challenging situations. A sound understanding of cultural safety, health inequities, systemic racism and the social determinants of health, and a commitment to fostering inclusive environments. Strong interpersonal, communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work effectively with students, colleagues and community stakeholders. Excellent organisational skills, sound judgement and the ability to manage competing priorities while maintaining confidentiality and professional boundaries. College of Medicine and Public Health            At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do. Life at Flinders We're transforming and investing in people and facilities to create contemporary, stimulating, and satisfying learning and work environments that reflect our core values of excellence, innovation, courage, and integrity. Flinders is refocusing its strategic priorities with the aim of elevating its performance to be a top ten Australian university, and amongst the top 1% in the world. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia) Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment A current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check which is satisfactory to the University will be required by Flinders University before the successful applicant can commence in this position. A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Karen Lower . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 14 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Lecturer-in-Exercise-Science_JR0000017340": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Exercise Science",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Continuing Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role The Lecturer in Exercise Science will be expected to contribute to the planning and delivery of topics within the suite of undergraduate and/or postgraduate courses. This includes making an independent contribution to the review, renewal, development and design of curricula and courses that are attractive to existing and new cohorts of students. The incumbent will also contribute to innovative research that aligns with the Caring Futures Institute (Australia’s first fully dedicated research centre for the study of self‐care and caring solutions). The role will provide a significant and high-quality research contribution to the Caring Futures Institute through collaboration, cross-disciplinary research activity, and the strengthening of existing and new partnerships with internal and external stakeholders. The lecturer will contribute to innovative research and evaluation through activities such as scholarship, quality publication, external grant acquisition and presentations. Please refer to the attached position description, for a comprehensive list of roles and responsibilities. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through education, research and partnership , opening opportunity, and translating knowledge into outcomes that strengthen communities About You You have relevant and extensive contemporary experience in Exercise Science/Exercise Physiology, with the demonstrated capacity to undertake high quality teaching in a tertiary environment, including experience in topic coordination and curriculum design. You communicate with clarity and impact, using your interpersonal skills to build productive relationships with colleagues, students, industry partners and community stakeholders. Your natural ability to collaborate and engage with relevant external professional domains will develop productive partnerships that can provide opportunities for students and promote graduate employability. Your keen attention to detail will allow you to prioritise tasks and meet deadlines while always demonstrating a commitment to high quality service delivery. You will also be able to demonstrate: Hold and or be eligible to maintain a current membership/registration with Exercise and Sports Science Australia. Evidence of a strong research track record (for career stage) in an area aligned with research strengths in Physiotherapy including evidence of quality publications and attraction of external competitive grants. Understanding of the nexus between teaching and learning, research, scholarship and practice. Experience and successful track record in supervision of honours and higher degree students. You will also have completed a PhD, or equivalent qualification in Exercise Sciences/Exercise Physiology or any one of the following in the attached position description. College of Health and Enablement  At the College of Health and Enablement, we aim to be an innovator in contemporary education producing a safer and more resilient health and social care workforce. Our mission is to improve lives and empower communities, enabling better health and caring in the world through an exceptional student learning experience. At the College, our research mission is about co-creating new knowledge which promotes wellbeing, and better care and support, to individuals and communities across the lifespan. We work proactively with industry partners and professional bodies leading to more employable and innovative graduates. Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide Ninety per cent of Flinders research has been ranked at ‘world standard or above’ by Excellence in Research for Australia (ERA). Wide range of professional development activities, funds and services, such as Flinders University’s Researcher Development and Leadership Programs. We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. SA: Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Professor Chris Barr Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 14 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Lecturer-in-Optometry_JR0000017337": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Optometry",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Continuing Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role We are seeking a Lecturer in Optometry (Teaching and Research), who will contribute to quality research, along with the planning and delivery of topics within the suite of undergraduate and/or postgraduate courses. The incumbent will develop teaching materials, deliver lectures, tutorials, examinations and placement education. The role will deliver inspiring and innovative teaching that enhances the student experience, fosters student engagement and supports students experiencing academic or other challenges that may impact their progress. The Lecturer will also contribute to the supervision and development of higher degree research students. The role will also be responsible for the planning and delivery of ethical, high quality and innovative research through activities such as quality publication, external grant acquisition, scholarship and presentation that aligns with the College’s areas of research strength. Please refer to the attached position description for a comprehensive list of roles and responsibilities This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through an ongoing contribution to improved teaching, learning & research outcomes for the university College of Health and Enablement  At the College of Health and Enablement, we aim to be an innovator in contemporary education producing a safer and more resilient health and social care workforce. Our mission is to improve lives and empower communities, enabling better health and caring in the world through an exceptional student learning experience. At the College, our research mission is about co-creating new knowledge which promotes wellbeing, and better care and support, to individuals and communities across the lifespan. We work proactively with industry partners and professional bodies leading to more employable and innovative graduates. About You You are an accomplished academic with a demonstrated capacity to deliver high-quality teaching in a tertiary environment, including experience in topic coordination, curriculum development and innovative approaches to teaching and learning. You have a strong research track record appropriate to your career stage, aligned with the research strengths of Optometry, supported by high-quality publications and a demonstrated ability to attract competitive external research funding. You understand and value the nexus between teaching and learning, research, scholarship and professional practice, and are committed to translating research into meaningful educational and clinical outcomes. You have excellent oral and written communication skills, and using your interpersonal skills, can build productive relationships with colleagues, students, industry partners and community stakeholders. In this role you will also be able to demonstrate: Relevant and extensive contemporary experience in Optometry. Hold and maintain a current membership/registration with Optometry Board of Australia & Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency. Completion of a PhD, or other relevant and equivalent qualification and/or experience: Evidence of peer-reviewed independent research demonstrated to be of an equivalent standard in the discipline area. Evidence of professional standing, reputation and qualifications demonstrated to be of an equivalent standard in the discipline area. Completion of a higher degree at master’s level or substantial progress towards a doctoral degree in the discipline area. Experience and successful track record in supervision of honours and higher degree students. Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide. Ninety per cent of Flinders research has been ranked at ‘world standard or above’ by Excellence in Research for Australia (ERA). Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities, funds and services, such as Flinders University’s Researcher Development and Leadership Programs. We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Professor Chris Barr Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 14 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
